# Phase 2 Deliverable 2 post-review loop implementation

This captures the implementation receipt for the HITL post-review loop closed
on branch `phase2-d2-postreview`.

## Decision

The architecture verdict held: Deliverable 2 is a driver plus operator
ingestion path. No branch was added to the core runner, supervisor Durable
Object, or memory-fabric cartridge.

## Built

- `scripts/workflow-app-submit-follow-up.ts` reads a
  `memory.hitl-follow-up-run-request.v1` artifact file, refuses non-`drafted`
  or already-submitted artifacts, probes `GET /runs/:runId/status` for
  idempotency, posts the artifact `request` to `POST /runs` only when the
  follow-up run is not already known, then polls to terminal status.
- `scripts/workflow-app-write-hitl-decision.ts` ingests a human-authored
  `memory.hitl-decision.v1` by validating it and writing it through an
  `ArtifactStoreContract`. The live CLI constructs the existing
  Cloudflare git-backed artifacts store; tests use the lifecycle-faithful fake
  store to prove the same write contract.
- The follow-up submission receipt includes `decisionSource` when the drafted
  request notes expose `generated-draft` or `human-review`.

## Receipts

- `tests/integration/dream-hitl-decision-seed-node.test.ts` now covers both
  post-review paths with `createLifecycleFaithfulArtifactsRemote`: generated
  draft and human review both seed, draft a follow-up request, submit to a
  captured terminal status, and preserve `decisionSource`.
- The generated-draft test reruns the submission driver against the same
  `submitted:false` draft and asserts only one `POST /runs` happened for the
  follow-up `runId`.
- The human-review test writes `report/hitl-decision.json` into the run's
  Artifacts repo via the ingestion helper before seeding the workflow, proving
  the human review branch stays a boring artifact write.

## Constraints Held

- Planner remains one-shot for the original run; the follow-up is submitted as
  a new `WorkflowRunRequest`.
- Draft-only side-effect posture is unchanged. The follow-up request carries
  planner notes that keep unleased updates as artifacts for human review.
- Customer-private boundary is preserved: no AIHero support data, private
  paths, credentials, or raw support threads were read or copied.
